    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. so basically the CNS is just fried. once we finish the deload, how do we actually structure the volume increase? like, do we just ramp the sets back up linearly or is there a better way to reintroduce the stress so they don't just hit another wall immediately?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A linear ramp-up after a deload is often too blunt an instrument; it's safer to reintroduce stress more strategically, like a carefully planned wave of small, targeted increases. You don't want to immediately throw them back into the grinder, which could cause them to crash right back into the plateau.\n\nInstead of just adding sets, consider undulating periodization, which means varying the intensity and volume across the week or even across the session. For example, you could start the first week post-deload with moderate volume and moderate intensity, focusing on perfect technique and feeling the muscle work, rather than chasing a PR.\n\nThen, you can introduce a block where you focus purely on increasing *time under tension* (TUT) with controlled eccentrics, which is a different type of stress than pure load, before finally ramping up the absolute load again. This gradual, varied approach allows the adaptations gained during the recovery to consolidate before the next major overload.",
      "source": "target",
